ook and twitter  compared to that stop kaveed  is not a threat michelle hopes more  people will use the app  and that the covet 19 epidemic soon  becomes a thing of the past  i'm joined now by ralph yanz he's the  founder of research to guidance  that's consulting firm for the digital  health industry in berlin  ralph it's good to have you with us  first of all now the german app came out  this week and it already has 10 million  downloads  does that make it a success  no it's definitely a very good start  also compared to  other official tracing apps  in other of other countries so  they have performed uh much worse  uh than uh here in germany so it's a  good start but  let's see how that further develops  we think that maybe um first movers have  now downloaded  it yeah and now becomes a hard part of  the game  to convince all the other ones the other  ones who are  a bit skeptical here to also download  and install this application but it's a  good start anyhow  now this app works without a centralized  data registry  should that reassure users about their  data security  yeah it's definitely a much secure in  terms of privacy  approach so yes uh  i mean the balancing of brighter data  privacy  and being able to fight  the pandemic with the help of a tracing  app we think  that the balance is being has been  reached and  we would recommend downloading the  application  as we heard earlier there are plenty of  other national apps france italy  um singapore for example how effective  is it going to be when people are  traveling across borders and they have  their own countries app but maybe not in  other countries  yeah that's right i mean that's still a  problem and  so if you met somebody who's affected in  another country  and he's using a different app then your  app wouldn't  wouldn't know about it yeah so still  our country apps are still not talking  to each other  that might take a while until those  solutions are going to be interoperable  but for now they